  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nTODAY:\n\nA low pressure system continues to drop down the West Coast \ntoday. This will transition the parent flow to southwesterly, \nwhich will advect upper level moisture into the region. This \nwill increase cloud cover over the coming days, but keeps the \nmoisture confined to those upper levels. Therefore, a pleasant \nfall day is on tap today.\n\nISOLATED TO SCATTERED SHOWERS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA wave rotating around the parent low will provide enough lift \nto kick off showers beginning Friday afternoon and lasting \nthrough Saturday. PWAT values are projected to be upwards of \n200% of normal, although dry surface levels will take time to \nfully saturate. Precipitation totals through Saturday will be on\nthe lighter side as a result, with predominantly rain showers, \nwith some snow flurries possible above 10,000 ft. There is a \npossibility for a few stray thunderstorms, which would be \ncapable of producing localized heavier rainfall across the \nregion. \n\nWETTER AND UNSETTLED BY SUNDAY AND MONDAY:\n\nA stronger moisture push associated with an atmospheric river \n(AR) arrives by Sunday. This AR will begin taking a southern \ntrack through Baja California, propagating through the Desert \nSouthwest. There continues to be disagreements between both the \nECMWF and GFS models. The ECMWF insists on bringing the moisture\nplume further north, while the GFS confines the highest PWATs \nto the Four Corners region. Therefore, there are still some \nuncertainties when it comes to the extent of this moisture. \nEither way, this is an impressive amount of moisture that, when \ncombined with enhanced lift ahead of an approaching low, will \nresult in widespread precipitation. Models do still agree on an \narea of 1-2 inches of rainfall across southwest Colorado and \nsoutheast Utah Sunday into Monday. Chances for river flooding \nremains low at this point, as these totals will be spread over \nan almost 48 hour period. The bigger concern lies with flash \nflooding across areas with already saturated soils from last \nweek's rainfall, particularly for areas of steep terrain, and \nover recent burn scars. See the Hydrology section below for \nadditional information. \n\nAdditionally, a cold front pushes through from northwest to \nsoutheast Sunday night into Monday, with snow levels dropping to\naround 8,000 ft by Monday morning, and close to 7,000 ft by the\nevening as colder air pushes behind the front. Snow could be \nheavy at times. Mountain passes will likely see impacts, with \nsome travel slowdowns for motorists Monday morning and into the \nafternoon. Precipitation will taper off Monday night, with \ncolder and drier conditions anticipated Tuesday and beyond. \nDaytime highs on Monday and Tuesday will run close to 10 degrees\nbelow normal, with temperatures remaining in this range through\nmuch of next week.",
